-- title:
--   Degree of ℚ(j)(j(q^N)) over ℚ(j) equals ψ(N)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let `data` be a `ModularPolynomialData N`, that is: a polynomial $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ which is monic in $Y$, whose $Y$-degree is $\mathrm{dedekindPsi}\,N = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$ (the Dedekind $\psi$-function), and which satisfies $\Phi(j(q), j(q^N)) = 0$ in the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$; here the coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ are evaluated by `evalAtJ`, the ring map sending $X$ to `jq` $= q^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{jNumQ}(q)$, the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant with its integral numerator power series mapped into $\mathbb{Q}$, and `jqN N` is the image $q \mapsto q^N$ of `jq` under the substitution `qExpand ℚ N` (induced by multiplication by $N$ on the exponent group $\mathbb{Z}$). Assume further `PhiIrreducible data`: the polynomial $\Phi$, with its coefficients pushed into the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(jq) \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ by `evalAtJGen`, is irreducible over $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$. Then the $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$-vector space dimension of $\mathbb{Q}(jq)\bigl(jqN\,N\bigr)$, the intermediate field generated over $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$ by `jqN N` inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$, equals $\mathrm{dedekindPsi}\,N$.
--
--   This is the classical statement that the modular equation of level $N$ realises $j(q^N)$ as an algebraic element of degree $\psi(N) = [\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}) : \Gamma_0(N)]$ over the field generated by $j$, conditional here on the irreducibility hypothesis packaged in `PhiIrreducible`. It is the exact-degree counterpart of the general upper bound for such adjunctions, and is used in establishing that $q$-expansion substitutions $q \mapsto q^c$ with $\gcd(c,N) = 1$ stay inside the relevant adjoined field.
